          },
          {
            "id": "p12-q10-short-method",
            "type": "paragraph",
            "fontSize": null,
            "align": "left",
            "spaceBefore": 0,
            "spaceAfter": 2,
            "lineHeight": 1.4,
            "indent": 0,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Use six 3s and one 2. Splitting larger terms and replacing three 2s by two 3s shows that no larger product is possible.",
                "marks": []
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      "worked": "Six $3$s and one $2$ have sum $20$ and product\n\n$$3^6\\times2=1458.$$\n\nTo prove maximality, first exclude $1$: combining it with any other term $a$ replaces the product factor $a\\times1$ by $a+1$, increasing the product without changing the sum.\n\nFor an even term $n=2k\\ge4$, replace it by $k,k$. Since\n\n$$k^2-2k=k(k-2)\\ge0,$$\n\nthe product does not decrease; equality occurs only when $n=4$.\n\nFor an odd term $n=2k+1\\ge5$, replace it by $k,k+1$. Since\n\n$$k(k+1)-(2k+1)=k^2-k-1>0\\quad(k\\ge2),$$\n\nthe product increases. Repeating these replacements leaves only $2$s and $3$s, with no smaller product.\n\nThree $2$s have sum $6$ and product $8$, whereas two $3$s have the same sum and product $9$. Thus a maximum has at most two $2$s. If there are $a$ copies of $2$ and $b$ copies of $3$, then\n\n$$2a+3b=20,\\qquad a\\in\\{0,1,2\\}.$$\n\nOnly $a=1$ makes $20-2a$ divisible by $3$, giving $b=6$. Therefore the maximum is $1458$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
